.TH STDIO 3
.SH NAME
stdio \- standard buffered input and output
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <stdio.h>

FILE *stdin;
FILE *stdout;
FILE *stderr;
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The standard I/O library reads and writes
.IR streams :
files opened with a buffer, so that a program can take or give one
character at a time without a system call for each. A stream is named
by a pointer to a FILE, the structure <stdio.h> defines;
.BR fopen (3)
returns one. Three streams are open when a program starts:
.TP 9
.B stdin
the standard input, file descriptor 0
.TP
.B stdout
the standard output, file descriptor 1
.TP
.B stderr
the standard error, file descriptor 2
.PP
A stream gets a buffer of BUFSIZ bytes (512) from
.BR malloc (3)
when it is first read or written, unless
.BR setbuf (3)
has given it one, or none. Output to a stream is kept in its buffer and
written when the buffer is full, when
.BR fflush (3)
or
.BR fclose (3)
is called, and when the program calls
.BR exit (2)
or returns from
.BR main .
.B stdout
is line buffered when it is a terminal: what is written to it goes out
at the end of each line, and also whenever the program is about to read
from
.BR stdin ,
so that a prompt is seen before the answer is typed. Written to a file
or a pipe, stdout is buffered fully.
.B stderr
has no buffer: each character goes out as it is written, so that
messages appear at once, in the right order.
.PP
A program that ends with
.BR _exit (2),
or is killed by a signal, loses what is still in its buffers.
.PP
At most _NFILE (20) streams can be open at once, the three standard ones
among them.
.PP
The constant EOF (\-1) is returned by the input functions at the end of
the file and after an error; NULL (0) is the null pointer.
.PP
The functions of the library, each described on its own page:
.TP 20
.B fopen
open a stream; also freopen, fdopen
.TP
.B fclose
close a stream; fflush writes out its buffer
.TP
.B getc
read a character; also getchar, fgetc
.TP
.B gets
read a line; also fgets
.TP
.B putc
write a character; also putchar, fputc
.TP
.B puts
write a string; also fputs
.TP
.B printf
formatted output; also fprintf, sprintf
.TP
.B scanf
formatted input; also fscanf, sscanf
.TP
.B fread
read or write blocks of bytes; also fwrite
.TP
.B fseek
move in a file; also ftell, rewind
.TP
.B ungetc
push a character back
.TP
.B ferror
a stream's error and end of file flags; also feof, clearerr, fileno
.TP
.B setbuf
give a stream a buffer of the program's, or none
.SH NOTES
getc, getchar, putc, putchar, feof, ferror, clearerr and fileno are
macros, defined in <stdio.h>. getchar and putchar are also functions,
for programs that do not include it.
.PP
The functions of the library and the system calls
.BR read (2)
and
.BR write (2)
should not both be used on the same file: the stream's buffer would
hold data the system calls do not see.
.SH FILES
.TP 26
/usr/include/stdio.h
the definitions
.TP
/usr/lib/cc/libc.a
the library
.SH SEE ALSO
open(2), close(2), read(2), write(2), malloc(3), intro(3)
.SH HISTORY
The early editions of AT&T UNIX had fopen, getc and putc, working on
buffers the program declared itself. The standard I/O library, with
FILE streams, <stdio.h>, and stdin, stdout and stderr, first appeared in
Version 7 AT&T UNIX.
